title: Opening a row
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: open-button double-click
summary: A click on a row selects it, and only that; opening is a button at the start of the row — ↘ goes into it, ↗ shows it in the file panel, ⓘ in the detail panel. Enter and Shift+Enter do the same. No double click.

A click on a row of a panel ''selects'' it, and does nothing else. ''Opening'' it is a
button at the start of the row, drawn larger than the text so it is easy to aim at:

* `↘` — go ''into'' the row: the listing then shows what it holds (a folder). Always ringed in the accent colour: it is the one way in, and must not be missed.
* `↗` — ''show'' the row in the [[file panel]] of the other slot: the file, or the folder as a grid.
* `ⓘ` — show the row in the [[metarecord-detail panel]] of the other slot: its fields.

`↗` and `ⓘ` are dim until the row is hovered or under the cursor. A row with nothing
for one of the two viewers has that button ''greyed'', and it
does nothing: `↗` on a metarecord without a file or folder, `ⓘ` on an entry of the
file manager no metarecord records (an untracked file). The buttons are styled in the
user stylesheet (`button.mf-open`, `button.mf-open-enter`, `button.mf-open-disabled`;
[[GUI configuration]]).

!! The keys

The keys open the same two viewers, whatever the panel:
<<key "panel:open other file" Enter>> shows the row under the cursor in the file panel,
<<key "panel:open other metarecord-detail" Shift+Enter>> in the detail panel — in the
other slot; after <<key "panel:open here file" "i">> in place of the list, after `b`
in a new workspace ([[open targets|Panel slots and layout]]). It is
[[panel:open]] itself, since the row under the cursor is already the workspace's
selection ([[Cross-panel selection]]): opening a viewer is opening a panel type, with
nothing of the list's own in between, so the panel opened is the one the key names —
never the detail panel ''instead of'' the file panel because the metarecord has no
file. `panel:open` refuses a viewer that has nothing of the selection to show, the
way the greyed button does, and says so.

Two panels give Enter a meaning of their own on some rows: the file manager goes into
a folder (<<key "file-manager:activate other" Enter>>, as `↘` does) and the duplicates
panel unfolds a group (<<key "duplicates:activate other" Enter>>); on a file and on a
copy they show it in the file panel like the others.

!! Why not a double click

The panels used to open a row on a double click. Two clicks on the same row is also how
text is selected — the browser selects a word on a double click, and a drag between the
two clicks selects some text or starts dragging the icon, and the second click is then
a click again, not the end of a double click. So selecting a name risked opening the
row, and a double click barely off the mark did nothing, which looked like a bug. A
button opens with one click, and the row's text can be selected and the row clicked as
often as wanted.

The button is `openButton` in `/__ui.js`, styled `button.mf-open` in the shipped
stylesheet; a panel's row only calls it with what opening does. Pressing it keeps the
focus where it was (the panel's keys keep working).

!! Where

|!Panel |!Button |!Opening |
|[[file-manager panel]] |`↘` on a folder, `↗` on a file and on `.`; `ⓘ` on every row |goes into the folder; selects the file (or the folder shown) and shows it in the file panel; `ⓘ` in the detail panel (greyed when untracked) |
|[[metarecord-list panel]] |`↗` and `ⓘ`, in a column of their own — under the thumbnail in the grid |shows the metarecord in the file panel (greyed without a file), in the detail panel |
|[[treeref panel]] |`↘` |goes into the node |
|[[log panel]] |`↘` on every row |goes into it: the list shows what a label or a fold of the watcher's flushes holds, and what an operation changed (it used to unfold in place — no reason for the log to unfold where the treeref panel goes into; an operation's changes had a panel of their own, `log-detail`) |
|[[recent panel]] |`↗` and `ⓘ` |shows the metarecord in the file panel, in the detail panel |
|[[duplicates panel]] |`▸`/`▾` on a group, `↗` and `ⓘ` on a copy |unfolds the group in place; shows the copy in the file panel, in the detail panel |
|[[trash panel]] |none |restoring writes to the disk: <<key "trash:restore" Enter>> or the right-click menu, never one click |
